        ]
      },
      "worked": "Assume $a,c>0$, $b>0$ and $b\\ne1$. If $c=1$, then $a^{\\log_b(1)}=a^0=1$ and $1^{\\log_b(a)}=1$.\n\nFor $c\\ne1$, use $a=c^{\\log_c(a)}$ and change the logarithmic base to $b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}a^{\\log_b(c)}&=\\left(c^{\\log_c(a)}\\right)^{\\log_b(c)}\\\\a^{\\log_b(c)}&=c^{\\log_c(a)\\log_b(c)}\\\\a^{\\log_b(c)}&=c^{\\frac{\\log_b(a)}{\\log_b(c)}\\log_b(c)}\\\\a^{\\log_b(c)}&=c^{\\log_b(a)}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
